  "semantic_checklist": [
    {
      "id": "C01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Attached printed pages 4–6 contain (1.10), the discussion of discontinuous solutions, normalized cell averages, and the Riemann workflow following (1.11).",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation addresses integral conservation, cell averages, adjacent-state inputs, numerical fluxes, and a single-step update.",
      "reasoning": "The selected passage and surrounding context match the subjects under comparison. Source hashes are supplied provenance; I did not independently recompute them."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The source discusses vector conservation laws, neighboring grid cells, and hyperbolic Riemann problems without specifying an integer-indexed global grid or a full-state-space differentiability domain.",
      "translation_evidence": "States are positive-dimensional finite real vectors; cells are indexed by all integers; the flux is differentiable and has a real-diagonalizable Jacobian at every real state.",
      "reasoning": "Finite real vectors fit the passage, but the global state domain and grid restrictions require justification beyond the recorded accuracy interpretation."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) concerns arbitrary spatial endpoints. The workflow concerns neighboring averages and a time step, without detailed quantification over traces or admissible solver inputs.",
      "translation_evidence": "Integral balance holds for every real space-time rectangle; every adjacent pair must belong to the method domain; derivative exceptional sets may depend on the spatial interval.",
      "reasoning": "The source does not resolve these global quantifiers and exceptional-set conventions. The recorded interpretation selects conditional error bounds but does not explicitly settle them."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The integral formulation accommodates discontinuities; sufficient smoothness is required specifically for deriving the classical differential equation.",
      "translation_evidence": "Every spatial slice and every pointwise physical-flux trace must be locally interval integrable, and rectangle balance must hold at all real endpoints and times.",
      "reasoning": "These assumptions do not impose smoothness on q, but they select a particular representative and trace interpretation that the source leaves unspecified."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "Section 1.2.1 defines a Riemann problem using the hyperbolic equation and the piecewise constant initial data (1.11), then obtains information by solving that problem.",
      "translation_evidence": "A problem stores two states for a fixed law. Result is arbitrary, and no condition relates solve or its output to the equation or its initial data.",
      "reasoning": "The translated pipeline records adjacent inputs and function composition but omits the substantive relationship between the routine and a Riemann problem. Allowing information-only approximate routines does not itself remove that relationship."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source defines cell averages by division by cell volume and uses left endpoint flux minus right endpoint flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "D004, D005, D021–D024, D029, and the integration dependencies explicitly explain normalization, oriented integrals, physical time averages, and the numerical update.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied dependency ledger gives concrete meanings for the relevant operators. Normalization and flux signs agree with the source; understanding an operator does not establish source authorization for every additional assumption."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) has inflow-minus-outflow sign. The recorded coordinator interpretation permits independent old-average and physical-face-flux error bounds implying a next-step bound.",
      "translation_evidence": "The weighted error equals the old weighted error plus the time increment times left flux error minus right flux error; the norm estimate is conditional on three non-strict bounds.",
      "reasoning": "Signs and conditional inequality strength match the recorded interpretation. This quantitative estimate is not presented as an explicit printed theorem."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source distinguishes conserved state, physical flux, approximate averages, Riemann information, and numerical flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "q, F, old, Q, Result, Information, H, G, and U retain separate roles; old need not equal Q(s).",
      "reasoning": "Exact and computed quantities are distinguished correctly. The missing correctness relation for Result is addressed separately under C05 and N01."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The Riemann inputs are q_l=Q_{i−1} and q_r=Q_i; cell averages divide integrals by cell volume.",
      "translation_evidence": "Interface j uses old(j−1) on the left and old(j) on the right. Cell i uses H_i and H_{i+1}, with h_i=R_i−L_i and time increment t−s.",
      "reasoning": "Neighbor ordering, interface offsets, volume normalization, and flux-difference signs agree."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The passage describes nonlinear hyperbolic systems and finite volume grids generally, without fixing the admissible state domain or global grid extent.",
      "translation_evidence": "The law is globally differentiable and pointwise real diagonalizable on all of V; the grid is a contiguous integer-indexed family without a coverage requirement.",
      "reasoning": "These are identifiable specializations or modeling choices. Their equivalence to the selected source setting is not established by the supplied pages or the recorded interpretation."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.11) leaves the initial value at x=0 unspecified; the source gives no zero-volume averaging convention.",
      "translation_evidence": "No initial value at the jump is imposed. Cell widths and t−s are strictly positive, and the relevant integrals are genuinely integrable.",
      "reasoning": "The translation does not introduce a source-mandated jump value or exploit totalized division and integration on degenerate averaging intervals. It still lacks the initial-data semantics identified in C05."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The source attributes useful interface information to solving a Riemann problem.",
      "translation_evidence": "Result and Information are arbitrary; only constant-state flux consistency constrains the pipeline. The error estimate assumes bounds on its actual flux errors.",
      "reasoning": "The assumptions are satisfiable, but the Riemann-specific conclusion can hold for a routine performing no Riemann computation. For a scalar identity flux, a routine returning only the left input and using it as the numerical flux satisfies constant consistency; the translated conditions alone do not establish any solution relationship. The conditional error identity remains valid independently of that missing relationship."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "Following (1.11), solving the neighboring-state Riemann problem supplies information for numerical flux computation and updating averages. Page 6 permits approximate Riemann solvers.",
      "translation_evidence": "H is numericalFlux composed with extract and solve, but solve has no equation, initial-data, approximation, or other correctness obligation.",
      "reasoning": "The computational data flow is represented, but its Riemann-solving semantics are not. The recorded convention removes a compulsory complete returned field; it does not explicitly authorize an arbitrary consistent map to count as a Riemann routine."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The printed passage selects no error norm. The recorded interpretation allows explicit error bounds while forbidding attribution of a chosen norm or order to the book.",
      "translation_evidence": "The bound uses the maximum norm on finite real state vectors, with componentwise vector arithmetic.",
      "reasoning": "The norm is explicit and mathematically coherent as an implementation of the recorded interpretation. It must remain an interpretation choice rather than a source-specified norm."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source gives qualitative numerical-flux accuracy; the recorded interpretation selects conditional old-average and time-averaged physical-face-flux errors.",
      "translation_evidence": "The conclusion bounds one updated cell-average error using its old error and two physical-flux time-average discrepancies.",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded interpretation, the error notions agree. No convergence, stability, or unconditional accuracy guarantee replaces the selected conditional statement."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Section 1.2 describes approximate cell averages as available numerical data and distinguishes numerical fluxes from correct physical fluxes.",
      "translation_evidence": "old is arbitrary numerical data; Q is an exact normalized average of q; H is computed from old; G is an exact time-averaged physical flux.",
      "reasoning": "The translation preserves these distinctions and introduces their comparison only through explicit error hypotheses."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage supplies no asymptotic expansion, remainder, rate, or limiting regime.",
      "translation_evidence": "The error identity and conditional estimate are exact finite-step assertions with no asymptotic terms.",
      "reasoning": "There are no asymptotic or higher-order expressions to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The passage refers to later chapters for Riemann theory and does not assert unconditional existence, uniqueness, or admissibility for arbitrary equations and states.",
      "translation_evidence": "A solver exists only on its supplied domain, with all constant pairs admitted. Its outputs have no required solution meaning; the law also satisfies global structural assumptions.",
      "reasoning": "There is no unsupported assertion of unique physical solutions, but the connection between domain membership, output existence, and actual Riemann solvability remains unspecified."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "Even under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, the translation does not establish that interface information comes from an exact or approximate Riemann routine for the hyperbolic equation and initial data (1.11). Its abstract pipeline requires only constant-state consistency, and its conditional error estimate applies independently of a Riemann-solving relationship. Information-only outputs are allowed by the convention, but absence of a complete field does not justify absence of all solution or approximation semantics."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, normalized averages and the conditional one-step error estimate have a coherent source-based motivation. However, the supplied evidence does not settle the global state-domain restriction, all-real-time rectangle and pointwise-trace conventions, or whether arbitrary consistent pipelines are intended to qualify as Riemann routines. The printed source alone supplies neither the quantitative theorem nor these precise analytic conventions. Further adjudication is needed before claiming this implication."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "missing-riemann-process-semantics",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 5 defines the Riemann problem as the hyperbolic equation together with (1.11), then obtains information by solving it. The recorded interpretation allows information-only exact or approximate routines.",
      "translation_evidence": "D001, D019, D025, and D028 describe paired states and an arbitrary dependent output pipeline, constrained by constant-state consistency but no relation to the equation or initial data.",
      "impact": "The translated statement can certify the same pipeline and conditional error estimate without establishing any Riemann-solving meaning. The recorded interpretation does not explicitly authorize this semantic weakening."
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "unresolved-effective-domain-and-traces",
      "source_evidence": "Printed pages 4–5 preserve integral conservation for discontinuous solutions without specifying function spaces, trace representatives, or temporal exceptional sets.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation assumes integrability of every pointwise flux trace and exact balance on every real space-time rectangle, with a globally differentiable hyperbolic flux on the full real state space.",
      "impact": "These choices may restrict the represented solution and law classes. Their source faithfulness is unresolved and cannot be treated as a stronger conclusion merely because they are additional premises."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-qualified-error-estimate",
      "source_evidence": "The source describes reasonable flux approximation qualitatively; the separate recorded convention selects independent old-average and time-averaged physical-face-flux bounds.",
      "translation_evidence": "The exact error decomposition and maximum-norm estimate use precisely those three error inputs, positive cell width, and positive time increment.",
      "impact": "This part coherently implements the recorded convention, with correct normalization and signs. Any eventual acceptance must explicitly retain that qualification and must not attribute the norm or quantitative bound to the printed passage."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The attached source pages support the conservation signs, normalized averages, neighboring-state order, and Riemann-information-flux-update workflow. Under the separately recorded coordinator interpretation, the translated conditional error estimate is coherent and preserves exact versus computed quantities. Nevertheless, the translation lacks a substantive Riemann-solving relationship, and its precise global analytic domains remain unresolved. Acceptance is therefore unwarranted on this evidence; adjudication must resolve the failed process-linkage checks and the uncertain domain conventions."
}
